        "Whether you are standing in summer heat or walking through a winter wind, the temperature deep inside your body stays remarkably steady, usually close to 37 degrees Celsius. This is not an accident. The body has a control system that works much like the thermostat* in a modern house, constantly comparing the actual temperature with a target and making small corrections.",
        "The center of this system is a tiny region deep inside the brain called the hypothalamus*. It receives information from sensors in the skin and from the blood flowing through the brain itself. When these signals show that the body is becoming too warm or too cold, the hypothalamus sends out instructions to various organs. Most of the time we are not even aware that this is happening.",
        "When the body gets too hot, two main responses begin. First, blood vessels close to the skin widen, so that more warm blood flows close to the air and loses heat. This is why people's faces often turn red after exercise. Second, sweat glands* release water onto the skin. As the sweat evaporates, it carries heat away. Sweating works best in dry air; in humid weather the sweat stays on the skin, and we feel hot and sticky instead of cool.",
